<h2>The background</h2>
<p>Vimalbai married Ramkishan in May 1995 and died in the matrimonial home in February 1996 — nine months later — against a backdrop of alleged dowry demands for a television, a tape deck, an industrial plot and Rs 50,000. The husband&rsquo;s version was that she died accidentally during intercourse following an epileptic seizure. The post-mortem recorded asphyxia due to throttling, and the medical jurist excluded an epileptic fit as the cause of the internal neck injuries. The trial court convicted under Sections 498-A and 304-B but acquitted under Section 302; the Bombay High Court, on the State&rsquo;s appeal in 2013, convicted under Section 302 and imposed life imprisonment. The matter then waited thirteen years in the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The Section 106 architecture</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning follows <em>Trimukh Maroti Kirkan</em>, the touchstone for deaths inside the matrimonial home. The prosecution proved the foundational circumstances: an unnatural death by throttling, inside the home, while the wife was in the husband&rsquo;s exclusive company. At that point Section 106 of the Evidence Act — whose counterpart is Section 109 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam — placed on the husband the burden of explaining facts especially within his knowledge.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;By virtue of Section 106 of the Evidence Act, the statutory burden rested heavily upon the appellant-Ramkishan&rdquo; &mdash; and his false, medically refuted account &ldquo;provides an additional incriminating link that completes the chain of circumstantial evidence&rdquo;.</p>
</div>
<p>The false explanation did double duty: it failed to discharge the Section 106 burden, and it became itself a circumstance against him. With consistent witnesses proving dowry-linked cruelty, and the death occurring within seven years of marriage, the unrebutted presumption under Section 113-B sustained the Sections 498-A and 304-B findings as well.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence in matrimonial-death cases.</strong> The explanation offered at the earliest stage is the case. A version contradicted by the post-mortem converts from defence into evidence for the prosecution. Where the medical picture is genuinely ambiguous, the defence must engage it with expert material, not narrative.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For prosecutors.</strong> Section 106 is not a shortcut: the foundational circumstances — unnatural death, exclusive company, proximate conduct — must first be proved by ordinary evidence. Once they are, the accused&rsquo;s silence or falsehood closes the chain.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On appellate reversals of acquittal.</strong> The case also illustrates that an acquittal under Section 302 at trial is not the end where the High Court, on the State&rsquo;s appeal, finds the circumstantial chain complete — and the Supreme Court will sustain a well-reasoned reversal.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Deaths of young wives inside matrimonial homes present the prosecution&rsquo;s hardest evidentiary terrain — no eyewitnesses, interested defence narratives, and medical evidence as the only neutral voice. The Section 106 framework, applied consistently from <em>Trimukh Maroti</em> to this judgment, is the law&rsquo;s structural answer. The case, arising from a 1996 death and finally resolved in 2026, also stands as a sobering marker of criminal appellate timelines: thirty years from death to finality, with the convict on bail for much of it and now directed to surrender.</p>
